WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.340
- It was, you know,

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.390
it was probably the most important

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.570
shift in my career because,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.550
not because of it started the notoriety

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:09.520
that I got on the internet,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.820
it's because YouTube selling to Google

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.393
for $1.7 billion, you know,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.210
there wasn't a single video on YouTube

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.280
that had a million views when I was on.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.633
There was no videos that
were 20 minutes long.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.350
YouTube literally flew me out

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.713
to explain why I was making
videos over 10 minutes long.

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:35.160
And when it sold, no question,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.880
that singular day was
the day that I realized,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.053
huh, I have some skill, you know?

00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:46.000
What it made me do was
look back and say, you know

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.550
launching an ecommerce website in 1996

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.750
was probably pretty progressive after all.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.930
Doing email marketing when nobody

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.650
was doing it was probably right,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.230
and buying those Google AdWords

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.160
the day Google AdWords came out,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.930
that was probably smarter than I thought.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.980
It was no question the tipping
point where I was like hey,

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.620
I have something else going on here

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.690
other than I'm a good wine merchant.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.170
This is more macro, and obviously,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.193
reading the articles of
that Google purchase,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.970
there were a couple that referenced,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.480
and it was the first
time I had ever seen it,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.440
this term angel investor.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.480
Because I think a lot of people,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.460
I think people kind of,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.650
the people that know my story,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.108
they don't realize how insular I was.

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:28.400
I wasn't in Silicon Valley.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.770
I was in New Jersey.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.810
I was in a cocoon of the wine business.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.770
I was super undereducated.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.920
So, I didn't even really
understand the world,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:37.893
to be very frank.

00:01:39.004 --> 00:01:42.290
For example, even a year later
when I invested in Facebook,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.170
had I known of even the most basic concept

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.830
of carrying people's money

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.150
and making a 20% on the
backend, I would've,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.360
I don't even want to know
what would have happened

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.400
because I basically bet my life
that Facebook was gonna win.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.707
And to just think about even getting

00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:03.550
$5 million of the liquor
industry's rich guys' and gals'

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.593
money and having another
million on the backend.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:08.930
Just to give you context,

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:13.510
that would've translated
into $180 million.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.550
I mean, it was,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.630
it's really fun for me to think about

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.880
the brilliance of the things
that I think I've done

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.340
and the insanity and basic-ness.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.600
And I think that there's a level of,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.350
and I'm still that guy right now.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.900
There's a deep commitment, an obsession,

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.740
to the end consumer and
almost nothing else.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.500
What that means is I've gotta go through

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.933
my own patterns to get educated.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.650
And so, yeah, I was on,
I was, I saw YouTube.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.050
It was three months old.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.120
Nothing was going on.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.510
There was maybe some random
article on like SlashDot,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.860
you know about it, back
in the day, nerd stuff.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.470
And I was like this is, this is it.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.640
This thing is it, and
then right behind that,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.540
I decided I would be an angel investor,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.140
and if I felt that
feeling of why I did ecom,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.160
email, Google AdWords, and YouTube,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.790
if I had that feeling,
really, I would compare it,

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.590
like the only comp that I've seen

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.150
that I've consumed is the way Clive Davis,

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.520
in his documentary, talked about,

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.330
you know, Mariah Carey or Celine Dion.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.130
I genuinely believe I trade on intuition

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.850
and ear and understanding
of what you're gonna do

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:20.800
before you do it,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.240
the way I'm gonna trade voice

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:23.963
going forward for the next decade.

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:27.430
That's how it happened.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.230
So, I started The Wine Show

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.769
and it changed the course of my career.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:34.207
- It was a dimly-lit room.

00:03:34.207 --> 00:03:37.050
I recently went back
and watched episode one.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.380
If you haven't seen it,

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.290
I highly recommend checking it out

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.970
just for its pure authenticity and rawness

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.970
of what it's like to get started
pioneering something new.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:50.340
Then to come to see you do a
thousand of those shows later,

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.610
which is a level of commitment.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.460
- Yeah, I mean, jumping
to number three, I mean,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.900
it's staggering to me that people

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.513
don't understand the
variable that hard work is.

00:04:03.058 --> 00:04:05.790
It's stunningly funny to me

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.050
that people don't want to acknowledge it.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.920
People love to talk about working smart.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:12.833
People love it,

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:14.763
and that's fine.

00:04:14.763 --> 00:04:16.170
I think that's exactly right,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.440
but you don't know a single person

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.090
that has ever become successful

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.510
without putting in an
obnoxious amount of work.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:24.650
It doesn't exist.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.400
You know people that have
wealth that were handed it,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.950
but you know nobody who's earned it

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.000
without putting in the work.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.510
And I think, you know, and
the reason I like it so much,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.270
even at the face of
pushback in today's very,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.967
live your life, work-life
balance environment,

00:04:38.967 --> 00:04:39.830
and I love it.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.110
I love it, I'm thrilled for that.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.443
You need to be self-aware.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.410
I love it 'cause it feels
the most controllable.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.760
It feels a hell of a
lot more exciting to me

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.610
giving advice and say if you're not happy,

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.333
work three more hours a day
than it is go get smarter.

00:04:56.985 --> 00:04:59.730
- What year was it that you
launched that first show?

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.490
- February 21st, 2006.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.310
Watching what's happening with
entrepreneurship right now

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:09.263
is very exciting and concerning
all at the same time.

00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:10.730
You know, I'm 42 years old.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.880
I was an immigrant from the Soviet Union.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.090
Education was the way out
and I was a D and F student.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.210
And so, basically, what
that meant if you grew up

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.020
in the '80s and early
'90s was every parent

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.730
and teacher thought I was gonna be a loser

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.260
because it was a binary
game of if you don't

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.620
go to a great school,
you can't win in life.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.100
It was just, you know, for
everybody over 40 in here,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.620
you understand that, it was binary.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.510
There was no conversation of entrepreneur.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:37.820
The first time, you know,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.130
entrepreneur's an extremely new term.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:44.090
Even I called myself a
businessman not an entrepreneur.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.330
Entrepreneur meant you were a loser,

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.400
like you had a card that said you were,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.080
it meant that you like lived
on a beach and had an idea.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.163
It was so terrible.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.360
And now everybody's trying to be one.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.480
And I think we're over
correcting in the same way

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.550
that entrepreneurs tried
to be good students

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.130
and that was a bad idea.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.000
You've got a lot of people

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.030
that should work at BlackRock

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.960
and are actually trying
to be entrepreneurs.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.890
They will fail because they're incredible

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.050
number threes, sevens, nines, 27s.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.280
Being a number one is a
totally different DNA trait.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.120
It means you love getting
punched in the mouth.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.160
It means you don't care about
what other people think.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.300
It means so many things
that we don't talk about,

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.940
and I think a lot of people will go into

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:31.000
very bad mental places in their mid...

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:31.833
First of all,

00:06:31.833 --> 00:06:33.060
if you look at the current state

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.940
of a 22-year-old entrepreneur,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.883
the scariest part is if
they are overeducated,

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.500
they're going in a system

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.280
that they've never had any real adversity.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.440
The game is so big.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.050
If you come from the right family,

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.480
you feel no feelings of pushback,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.190
and then you go into the wild

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.330
and you've got a business, and the market,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.030
the market doesn't give a
fuck about your grandfather.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.900
And I'm watching it every
day and it's not fun.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.400
It's not fun for a 24-year-old,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.600
11 months in, realizing that they're soft

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.690
and not as smart as
they thought they were,

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:11.490
and their idea of building

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.570
the Uber for laundromats is a bad idea.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:17.500
I've been chasing attention my whole life.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:18.580
This isn't new.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.320
This isn't email and Google.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:22.780
This is, I used to do that.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.970
I would literally try to figure out

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.770
physically with your eyes as a driver,

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.400
like if you were looking at that tree

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.359
or that tree or that post.

00:07:31.359 --> 00:07:33.040
And then I thought oh my God,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.320
that's what I did at
baseball card shows at 11.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.960
That's why I didn't set up my table

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.430
for the first four hours
while everybody else did

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:42.090
and would walk the floor

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.090
and watch what people were reacting to.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.810
So, that I would go back
and set up my table.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.190
Oh shit, that's why I dominated
in my dad's liquor store

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.810
because when I was 14 and I
stood behind the register.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.520
I would watch people walk into the store

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.340
and how they would navigate.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.970
Oh shit, that's why my
websites did so well

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.931
because I was doing UI and UX

00:08:01.931 --> 00:08:04.450
before I realized what UI and UX was,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.740
and it all came back,

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.280
and probably in the
last four or five years,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.400
I finally articulated, which
is like fuck, it's all empathy.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:12.600
This is all empathy.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:13.700
I'm a good leader.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:15.040
I have a good community.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.930
I'm great with consumers because
I deploy empathy at scale.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:22.100
It is always about them, never about me.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.130
You want me to save you
a lot of time, BlackRock?

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.250
You know why you can't get
people interested in wealth?

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:28.883
'Cause it's about you not them.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.610
You're trying to figure out how to make it

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.610
valuable for you to get
them interested in it

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:36.733
and what's in it for you.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.100
The end.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:42.390
You haven't reversed engineered
what they give a shit about,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.140
and then all those people in that video,

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.180
that's cute in front of a camera,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.440
but that's not the truth of how

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:50.640
they're gonna navigate
their lives actually.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.816
- Okay, so, um, flowers

00:08:53.816 --> 00:08:56.813
(audience laughs)

00:08:56.813 --> 00:09:01.481
to lemonade to baseball
cards to a slight tangent,

00:09:01.481 --> 00:09:03.730
people that see BlackRock videos,

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:07.530
eventually to wine and then to, I mean,

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:11.630
and I love this evolution
from Gary Vaynerchuk

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.020
to Vayner to just Gary Vee.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.550
- Yeah.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.043
- Just, like, where did
you get your hustle?

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:20.393
- I think work eth...

00:09:20.393 --> 00:09:22.410
You know, I'm, again,
I'm not a great student,

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.360
but it seems to me, at least in the ethos,

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.500
that a lot of people agree
that work ethic is taught.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.720
And so, that's very easy.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.620
My parents.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:31.453
I mean,

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:33.960
it's such a big deal.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.063
It's such a big deal.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:37.470
I mean, I didn't even see my dad.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.340
My dad and I slept in the same roof

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.120
every day of my life for the first

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.133
18 years of my life, every day.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:45.950
We just didn't,

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.030
we took one family vacation.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.423
My dad never traveled once,

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.300
and I never saw him
until I was 14 years old.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.110
He left before I woke up.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:57.310
He got home after I fell
asleep, and he was a merchant.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:58.143
Right?

00:09:58.143 --> 00:09:58.976
He left at 7:00.

00:09:58.976 --> 00:09:59.810
He got home at 11:00 p.m.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.290
That was his life, and
he worked every day.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.600
My parents are from the Soviet Union.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.760
So, even though they grew
up in the '80s and '90s,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.490
which is still more common than today,

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:11.440
but they lived that classic
1947 separate life, right?

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.350
Like completely separate,
like two separate entities.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.530
My mom raised the three of us.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:20.890
We never had, there was, you
know, my mom did everything.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.680
I don't even know how to
do laundry to this day.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.250
She did absolutely everything.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.703
She worked from the moment she woke up

00:10:27.703 --> 00:10:28.950
'til the end of night.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:29.783
And so, my parents--

00:10:29.783 --> 00:10:31.740
- Is she still doing the laundry?

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.200
- I wish.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.000
No, but you know, I think, I think,

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.110
I think work ethic, my
hustle comes from my parents.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:39.800
It's very clear.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:41.440
I mean,

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:42.273
you know, it's funny.

00:10:42.273 --> 00:10:44.790
Me, my mom and dad, when
it's just the three of us,

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.730
not my sister and brother,
which happens rarely,

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.450
but if we are, somewhere
within the first hour,

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.600
we'll start making fun of each other

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:54.450
in such a loving way which is like fuck,

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.460
we're just a bunch of working dogs.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:57.293
You know?

00:10:57.293 --> 00:10:58.126
Like we just work.

00:10:58.126 --> 00:10:59.490
Like,

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.790
you know, and then I'm competitive

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.440
with my dad and him with me.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.410
And so, he worked so much.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:07.210
So, that I think, you know,

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:08.700
I have the luxury of the internet

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.710
and travel to outwork my dad, right?

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.890
Which has been like a really
funny thing with me and him

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.950
in the last five years which is him

