00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:47.290
is most of them play in
a Wall Street environment

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:50.010
and they do nothing
but short-term behavior

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:53.130
which is defense in the
face of the biggest change

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:54.090
in our society.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:59.090
It is laughable that Venmo was not created

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:00.603
by a financial service.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:07.540
It is laughable for financial
services to be as naive

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:12.540
as they are about Blockchain
and what that actually means.

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:14.730
It's gonna eliminate all your margins.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:17.340
I mean, you know, look,

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:21.180
what's not working is not
unique to financial services.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:23.000
Anybody that is in a lead position

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:27.290
financially in the world today
has the resources tied up

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:29.513
in something that has lost its leverage.

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:34.910
And so, that's a problem.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:37.053
And here's what I think is funny.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:41.200
Why are we so entitled to have the world

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:44.320
at a standstill during our lives?

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.270
Meaning, I want you to level up

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:47.350
what I'm actually talking about

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.950
which is why do we get to have peace

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:53.080
and why do we have to get continuity

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:55.210
financially or economically?

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:58.200
Like why are we not
supposed to be a generation

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.150
that goes through a great depression?

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:02.123
Why, why, why, why?

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:07.450
In America, I'm like, why
can't we be the generation

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:08.950
that watches our empire falling?

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:10.540
I mean, anybody that doesn't understand

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:13.760
that China is gonna win is just naive.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.633
This place used to be number one.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.800
Then America became number one.

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:21.730
And the same reason this
place lost its leadership

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:24.270
is the same reason America
is gonna lose its number one.

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:25.893
It's called entitlement.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.040
It's called too much prosperity

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:32.110
which then creates a
generation that doesn't know

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:33.380
how to fight for it.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:37.350
And so, that's more on a country basis.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.950
I think this is a macro
thing lifted above all that.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:42.983
And so, you know, look,

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:50.550
my friends, when you make
money by being in the middle

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:53.470
and the Internet and Blockchain come along

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:56.023
and they're the actual
middle, you're in trouble.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.050
So, you need to figure out what the hell

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.690
you're actually doing in providing value.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:05.100
And the fact that you amassed a mote

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:08.320
that gave you an advantage to,

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:11.850
and that became your middle,
that's a bad business

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.370
when that doesn't bring any value anymore.

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:17.540
So, let me tell you what
I do think is working

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:20.240
and I do think BlackRock has
done this incredibly well

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:23.980
and I am sure for the
most financially rigorous,

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:28.810
it has been something that
you have looked down on

00:25:28.810 --> 00:25:30.770
because you don't understand
what's actually happening,

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:32.950
which is, I think what
BlackRock has done well,

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:35.170
let's speak about BlackRock specifically,

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:36.913
is it's actually built a brand.

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:39.360
And if you actually understand

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:40.810
what's happening in technology,

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.500
you will realize very quickly that brand

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:46.003
is the only thing left.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:52.590
And so, I think that it's given
the organization permission

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:57.080
to create other services if
they actually understand.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:26:00.110
The problem is companies
in financial service,

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:01.410
like every other company,

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:03.240
and Chase is a big
client of ours in the US,

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:05.310
and we do some work with RBS here,

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:10.310
I mean, it's just so obvious,
you can't hold onto things

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:14.170
that are high margin when you
already see the alternative

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:18.083
in your face for the consumer
and that's the vulnerability.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:21.730
- What about other brands
outside of BlackRock

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:22.563
in the industry?

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:25.290
- I think everybody's
doing the same thing,

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:26.870
including BlackRock.

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:30.940
I think that there is a tone-deaf nature

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:33.360
to the way we communicate to consumers

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:37.420
that comes from an ivory tower
and is completely predicated

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:41.060
on self-interest and then,
even worse creatively,

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:44.980
sits with five or six
people who love the power

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:47.190
of having their subjective point of view

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:50.100
be the king or queen of the decision

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:53.780
who then also are putting
out very safe content

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:55.880
and vanilla content which ends up

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.420
meaning nothing to anybody.

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.990
And, because they're scared to lose,

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:04.980
do anything that would risk
this cushy life they have

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:08.480
to be the person that says
the video is nice or not.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:09.313
- Gotcha.

00:27:09.313 --> 00:27:11.160
Okay, let's open it up to the room

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:15.340
and I do see Mark Adams
almost in the front row

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:17.900
who's probably bound to have a question,

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:18.790
not to put you on the spot.

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:20.260
But I see other hands going up as well

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:22.980
so let's just start
right there on the left

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:25.180
or straight to Mark.

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:28.650
- [Man] Hi, Gary, I'm laughing
here because this is fire.

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:29.683
- Thank you, man.

00:27:29.683 --> 00:27:33.170
- [Man] Bro, like, I love the way

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:34.923
you talk about things that,

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:40.930
I feel like you're very much
into democratizing power

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:44.280
and I want you to go there for a second.

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:45.710
Just talk about that for a second.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:46.780
You talk about entitlement

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:48.817
and the fact that you were
coming from the Soviet Union

00:27:48.817 --> 00:27:52.060
and the hustle, and I love
that because it goes against,

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:54.650
you know, we live in a class society here

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:58.657
where certain things are
about who your grandfather was

00:27:58.657 --> 00:28:01.510
and could you maybe elaborate
a little bit more on that?

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:02.343
- You know what's funny?

00:28:02.343 --> 00:28:03.820
It's not an ideology.

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:05.483
It's a practicality.

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:07.530
I'll be very honest with you,

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:11.280
I have a funny feeling if
I was born 40 years ago,

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:13.533
I would be excited about classism.

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:19.410
I'm not excited about the
democratization of everything.

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:21.290
I just know it's happened.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.070
It's called the Internet.

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:26.320
Sorry, like it's over.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:29.060
Do you understand this has just started?

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:31.720
If you're on defense to
what's been happening,

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.290
right, from a classism or I've put my...

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:35.550
Retail.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.003
If you have 8,000 leases.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:42.940
Everybody talks about
retail is dead, TV is dead,

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.060
and then everybody will
counter with, yeah,

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:47.480
it's so dead that it's still
doing 80% of the retail.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:50.730
They don't understand when
you go from doing 100%

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:53.230
of the retail to 80% of the retail,

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:56.190
when you run an actual P and
L, all it needs to do is go

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:59.300
to 79% for the whole thing to break

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:01.800
because your leases then can't support,

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:04.223
your cogs break.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:08.150
When you can't get out of your lease,

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:11.250
but your foot traffic goes down by 1%,

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:12.400
you're out of business.

00:29:13.836 --> 00:29:16.510
I think because of the way I communicate

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:20.090
and because of my jersey-ness
and my immigrant-ness,

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:22.600
I don't think people pay
attention to what I'm talking,

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:25.930
I think they downsize the
complete and utter understanding

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:29.350
I have for psychology and business.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:30.183
And I love that,

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:34.300
I probably manifest and
force being underestimated

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:35.623
because it's an advantage.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:39.970
Ask me why I'm willing
to be self-promotional,

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.120
but you know very little
about VaynerMedia.

00:29:43.582 --> 00:29:45.300
It's 'cause I'm smart,

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:46.650
'cause I didn't want
anybody to know anything

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:48.970
about VaynerMedia cause
it's way more dangerous

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:50.880
than the incumbents realize

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:53.360
and the more I can make them
think that I'm a charlatan,

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:55.790
the more time I have to
fucking destroy them.

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:57.830
(audience laughing)

00:29:57.830 --> 00:30:00.400
- Let's go to the next question.

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:02.030
- Gary.
- Yes.

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:02.973
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:05.060
- Big fan.
- Thank you.

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:07.040
- [Man] I think you're
probably one of the best

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.300
content marketers out there

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:10.750
just because you're so damned efficient.

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:11.583
- Thanks.

00:30:11.583 --> 00:30:13.330
- [Man] You got a podcast
which you used to create a book

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:15.040
which has a hashtag as a name.

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:16.340
Wonderful.

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.410
In AskGaryVee, #AskGaryVee,

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:20.260
you talk a lot about clouds and dirt.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:21.460
- Yes.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:22.757
- [Man] I think clouds is thinking big,

00:30:22.757 --> 00:30:25.170
but I think dirt is the work

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.120
which you also talk a lot about, right.

00:30:28.031 --> 00:30:30.620
I'm really curious, if you
could just give me an example

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:33.500
of what do you consider to be the work

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:34.790
and what is the right work?

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:38.450
How can people differentiate
between just monotony

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:40.230
and doing the right work?

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:41.530
- That's a great question.

00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:45.010
Let me give you a clouds and dirt thing.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.860
I decide that voice is the next frontier.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:50.610
The dirt is I do a podcast.

00:30:50.610 --> 00:30:52.310
We build an Alexa skill.

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:54.180
I spend tons of time watching

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:55.750
how people react to other Alexas.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:57.560
I fly to Seattle for two hours

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:00.330
just to meet the tech stars Alexa team

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:03.373
on my dime, which is rare
these days, and fly back.

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:07.620
I follow up my macro
thesis with micro work

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.510
to close the gaps because what I realized

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:12.020
over the last 20 years is, you
know how many people in here

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:14.340
have opinions about things
based on headline reading

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:15.390
not practitionership.

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:18.750
I don't want that vulnerability.

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.100
I don't like when I hang
out with very smart people

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:22.360
and then I realize they have no idea

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.700
what they're talking about in
the area that I understand.

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:26.860
And I knew this about wine.

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:30.070
Growing up in the wine business,
I became deeply educated

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:32.570
and, all of a sudden, I started
meeting fancy three-star

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:35.140
Michelin sommeliers and
I realized, actually,

00:31:35.140 --> 00:31:37.660
he or she doesn't know what
the fuck they're talking about.

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:41.570
They're using their brand, their status,

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:42.620
but they're headline.

00:31:45.305 --> 00:31:46.330
So much bravado.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:47.593
I stay in my lane.

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:50.810
There's a ton of stuff around wealth

00:31:50.810 --> 00:31:54.040
that I can never talk about
at anywhere close to the level

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:56.220
that the people in this room understand.

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.460
Right, so I stay in my lane.

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:01.440
The right work is very subjective.

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:03.130
For example, all my fancy friends

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:05.560
think me community managing
and replying to everybody

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:08.500
on Instagram is the stupidest
use of my two hours.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:11.240
I think it's the reason I'm
the best consumer researcher

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:12.680
in the world.

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:13.790
Who's right, who's wrong?

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:14.780
I don't know.

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:16.280
It all plays out in the end.

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:19.320
I think what we need to do
is not judge ourselves overly

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:21.100
in small pockets.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:23.160
I think people start dwelling,
when they look back and say,

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.380
oh, the last three months,
I wasn't doing smart work.

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:27.563
It's three months out of a 100 years.

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:31.360
So, I think judgment on one self

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.680
is an important conversation around this

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:36.710
otherwise you start dwelling
on inefficient work.

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.010
It's gotta be thoughtful, but
I think people get caught up

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:40.568
in like, is this smart?

00:32:40.568 --> 00:32:41.520
You know, I mentioned it earlier.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:43.910
I don't know, I'm a big fan of offense.

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:45.490
I used this analogy last night.

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:47.500
In proper football terms,

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:50.220
I want my team to win nine to seven,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:51.830
which is an unusual score.

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:53.360
And when I talk about it in the US,

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:56.970
I talk about 157 to 142 in basketball.

00:32:56.970 --> 00:33:00.400
That's who I am and I think
that is a very good model

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.100
because what you realize is,

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.850
that means you don't respect defense.

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:06.180
And, when you don't respect defense,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:08.710
it's actually another way to say, hey,

00:33:08.710 --> 00:33:11.430
everybody who's a CFO-centric
company in the world

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:13.730
and, let me save you
time, that's all of them,

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:17.620
like CEO and CFO dynamics are funny,

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:18.970
I was like, wait a minute,

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:20.450
this is only the last three years,

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:23.148
I'm like wait a minute,
CEOs don't run companies,

00:33:23.148 --> 00:33:25.090
CFOs run companies.

00:33:25.090 --> 00:33:27.063
I'm like, that's stupid.

00:33:27.063 --> 00:33:28.350
(audience laughing)

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:29.183
So.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:30.863
- Yup.

00:33:30.863 --> 00:33:31.933
More hands going up.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:33.840
- Yup, she's in the back.

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:34.860
Hi.
- Hi.

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:35.693
- What's your name?

00:33:35.693 --> 00:33:38.216
- [Jasmine] Ah, it's Jasmine.
- Jasmine.

00:33:38.216 --> 00:33:39.710
- [Jasmine] I was curious
about what you said earlier

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:43.030
about how we're going to
all move to Alexa, Siri,

00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:44.630
all of these types of technologies,

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:46.250
and with the backlash that's happened

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:48.220
in terms of privacy terms,

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:49.620
where do you think the opportunities are,

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:51.250
because do you think this is just a blip

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:53.410
where everyone suddenly thinks,
oh, I don't want this thing

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.360
listening on all of my
private conversations

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:57.490
or are people en masse
going to say, actually,

00:33:57.490 --> 00:33:59.093
I don't really care if companies

00:33:59.093 --> 00:34:00.800
own all of my private information?

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.150
- Number two.

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.090
- [Jasmine] So, you
think people won't care?

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:05.510
- I know people don't care.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:06.590
- [Jasmine] I know but do
you think they will continue

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:07.810
because, I think, especially,

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:09.440
in terms of younger demographics,

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:11.570
people have more of an awareness about it.

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:13.762
Do you think that's just going
to be entirely overridden?

00:34:13.762 --> 00:34:14.595
- Correct.

00:34:14.595 --> 00:34:15.428
- [Jasmine] Okay.

00:34:15.428 --> 00:34:16.932
- Yeah, I think it goes.

00:34:16.932 --> 00:34:17.827
(audience laughing)

00:34:17.827 --> 00:34:19.950
I actually think it goes
way further than that.

00:34:19.950 --> 00:34:22.540
I actually think the
greatest thing in the world

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.338
would be for all of you to
realize there's no privacy.

00:34:25.338 --> 00:34:26.171
- [Jasmine] No, no, I think people realize

00:34:26.171 --> 00:34:28.990
